      { title: "Ancient Egypt: Gift of the Nile", emoji: "🏺", content: "Egyptian civilization grew along the Nile River, which flooded yearly and deposited rich soil for farming. Egyptians built the pyramids — massive stone structures that still stand after 4,500 years. They developed hieroglyphics (picture writing), made papyrus (an early form of paper), and practiced mummification to preserve the dead. Pharaohs were god-kings who ruled with absolute power. Egyptian civilization lasted over 3,000 years." },
      { title: "Why Rivers Mattered", emoji: "🌊", content: "Both civilizations arose along rivers because rivers provided water for drinking and irrigation, fertile soil for farming, transportation routes for trade, and fish for food. Once people could grow more food than they needed, not everyone had to farm — some could become craftspeople, priests, scribes, soldiers, or rulers. This specialization is what made civilization possible. Reliable food plus free time equals innovation." },
      { title: "Legacy That Lasts", emoji: "📜", content: "We still use inventions from these civilizations every day. Writing allows us to record and share knowledge. Mathematical systems make science and engineering possible. Legal codes form the basis of modern law. Calendars organize our time. The wheel enables transportation. These ancient peoples solved problems that still define how civilization works. Atlas says: understanding where civilization began helps you understand everything that came after." }
